Title: The Velveteen Rabbit
Credit: Written by
Author: Margery Williams
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 7/30/2026

FADE IN:

INT. NURSERY - CHRISTMAS MORNING

A warm, period live-action nursery glows with firelight. In the Boy's stocking sits a fat, bunchy VELVETEEN RABBIT: spotted brown-and-white coat, pink sateen ears, thread whiskers, and a holly sprig between his paws.

The BOY lifts the Rabbit from the stocking and hugs him amid oranges, nuts, a toy engine, and a clockwork mouse.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
There was once a velveteen rabbit, and in the beginning he was really splendid.

Aunts and uncles arrive with parcels. Tissue paper rustles. The Boy turns eagerly to newer gifts, leaving the Rabbit on the nursery floor.

INT. NURSERY - NIGHT

The Rabbit lies near the fender among proud mechanical toys, a chipped MODEL BOAT, and TIMOTHY, a jointed wooden lion.

At his side rests the SKIN HORSE, old and gentle, his brown coat bald in patches and his tail nearly gone.

RABBIT
What is REAL? Does it mean having things that buzz inside you and a stick-out handle?

SKIN HORSE
Real isn't how you are made. It's a thing that happens to you. When a child loves you for a long, long time, not just to play with, but REALLY loves you, then you become Real.

RABBIT
Does it hurt?

SKIN HORSE
Sometimes. When you are Real you don't mind being hurt.

RABBIT
Does it happen all at once, like being wound up, or bit by bit?

SKIN HORSE
It doesn't happen all at once. You become. It takes a long time.

The Skin Horse smiles at the Rabbit's anxious face.

SKIN HORSE
Generally, by the time you are Real, most of your hair has been loved off, and your eyes drop out, and you get loose in the joints and very shabby.

RABBIT
I suppose you are real?

SKIN HORSE
The Boy's Uncle made me Real. That was a great many years ago; but once you are Real you can't become unreal again. It lasts for always.

INT. BOY'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

NANA searches impatiently for the Boy's missing china dog. She spots the open toy cupboard and snatches the Rabbit out by one ear.

NANA
Here, take your old Bunny! He'll do to sleep with you!

She drops the Rabbit into the Boy's waiting arms.

The Boy hugs him tightly beneath the bedclothes. The Rabbit is nearly buried under the pillow, then peeks out as the Boy whispers.

BOY
This is a tunnel. It is like the burrows the real rabbits live in.

The Boy sleeps with both hands clasped around the Rabbit.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And so time went on, and the little Rabbit was very happy.

The Rabbit's fur grows worn. His tail comes unsewn. The pink rubs from his nose where the Boy kisses him.

EXT. GARDEN - SPRING DAY

The Boy pushes the Rabbit in a wheelbarrow past flower borders and raspberry canes. He builds a fairy hut around him.

Later, dusk settles. The Rabbit lies wet with dew on the lawn, earthy from a flower-bed burrow.

Nana carries a candle through the garden and finds him.

NANA
You must have your old Bunny! Fancy all that fuss for a toy!

INT. BOY'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

Nana holds the damp Rabbit away from the bed. The Boy sits upright and reaches for him.

BOY
Give me my Bunny! You mustn't say that. He isn't a toy. He's REAL!

Nana gives him the Rabbit. The Boy folds him beneath his chin.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
When the little Rabbit heard that, he was happy, for he knew that what the Skin Horse had said was true at last.

Morning light reveals the Rabbit's dim boot-button eyes and shabby velvet face. Nana studies him despite herself.

NANA
I declare if that old Bunny hasn't got quite a knowing expression!

EXT. WOOD - SUMMER EVENING

In a bracken nest, the Rabbit watches ants cross his velvet paws. The Boy runs off among the trees to pick flowers.

Two sleek, living WILD RABBITS creep from the fern. Their noses twitch at the toy.

WILD RABBIT
Why don't you get up and play with us?

RABBIT
I don't feel like it.

The Wild Rabbit hops sideways, then rises on his hind legs.

WILD RABBIT
Can you hop on your hind legs?

RABBIT
I can jump higher than anything!

WILD RABBIT
Then stretch them out and show me, like this!

The Wild Rabbit whirls and dances. The Rabbit sits rigid, his soft one-piece back hidden in the bracken.

RABBIT
I don't like dancing. I'd rather sit still!

The Wild Rabbit stretches close. His whiskers brush the Rabbit's ear. Then he recoils.

WILD RABBIT
He doesn't smell right! He isn't a rabbit at all! He isn't real!

RABBIT
I am Real! I am Real! The Boy said so!

Footsteps approach. The Boy runs past. The wild rabbits vanish in a flash of white tails.

RABBIT
Come back and play with me! Oh, do come back! I know I am Real!

Only ants move through the grass. The bracken sways softly.

INT. BOY'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

Weeks pass in a feverish sickroom. The Boy lies flushed and delirious, clutching the Rabbit under the bedclothes.

A lamp burns through the night. The Rabbit's threadbare coat presses against the Boy's hot cheek.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Strange people came and went in the nursery, and through it all the little Velveteen Rabbit lay there, hidden from sight under the bedclothes.

The Boy weakly opens his eyes. The Rabbit nestles at his pillow beside a picture book.

INT. BOY'S BEDROOM - MORNING

The recovered Boy sits wrapped in a shawl beside wide-open windows. The Rabbit lies tangled in the bedclothes.

The DOCTOR speaks quietly with Nana as trunks are packed for the seaside.

DOCTOR
The room is to be disinfected, and all the books and toys that the Boy has played with in bed must be burnt.

Nana notices the old Rabbit.

NANA
How about his old Bunny?

DOCTOR
That? Why, it's a mass of scarlet fever germs! Burn it at once. What? Nonsense! Get him a new one. He mustn't have that any more!

The Rabbit listens, hopeful at first, then still with dread.

EXT. GARDEN BEHIND THE FOWL-HOUSE - NIGHT

A sack of old picture books and rubbish lies beside the raspberry canes. The Rabbit wriggles his head through its untied opening.

Moonlight catches his faded brown spots and frayed whiskers. Beyond him, the familiar garden is silent.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Of what use was it to be loved and lose one's beauty and become Real if it all ended like this?

A real tear runs down the Rabbit's shabby velvet nose and falls into the earth.

A slender green stem rises where it lands. A golden cup of a flower opens.

The NURSERY MAGIC FAIRY steps from it, radiant in pearl and dew-drops, flowers at her throat and in her hair. She gathers the Rabbit into her arms and kisses his wet nose.

FAIRY
Little Rabbit, don't you know who I am?

RABBIT
No.

FAIRY
I am the nursery magic Fairy. I take care of all the playthings that the children have loved.

RABBIT
Wasn't I Real before?

FAIRY
You were Real to the Boy, because he loved you. Now you shall be Real to every one.

The Fairy holds him close and rises into the moonlit air.

EXT. WOOD GLADE - NIGHT

The Fairy descends into a silvered clearing. Wild rabbits dance on velvet grass, then stop in a ring around her.

FAIRY
I've brought you a new playfellow. You must be very kind to him and teach him all he needs to know in Rabbit-land, for he is going to live with you for ever and ever!

She kisses the Rabbit once more and sets him down.

FAIRY
Run and play, little Rabbit!

The Rabbit sits frozen, ashamed to show his missing hind legs.

Something tickles his nose. He lifts a hind toe to scratch it.

He stares. He has hind legs.

His dingy velveteen has become soft, shining brown fur. His ears twitch. Long whiskers brush the grass.

The Rabbit leaps. Then he springs sideways, whirls, and races with the wild rabbits through the moonlit glade.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He was a Real Rabbit at last, at home with the other rabbits.

EXT. WOOD - SPRING DAY

The Boy, healthy again, plays among the bracken behind the house.

Two rabbits creep out to watch him. One is brown all over. The other is brown with faint old spots showing beneath his fur.

The Boy sees the spotted rabbit and grows still.

BOY
Why, he looks just like my old Bunny that was lost when I had scarlet fever!

The Real Rabbit watches the Boy for one quiet moment, then turns and bounds after his companion into the bright wood.

FADE OUT.
